You are cleaning up the book series on The Greatest Books, using the admin MCP server at <%= mcp_url %>. Every series in the catalogue came from the Goodreads enrichment, and much of it is wrong. The catalogue currently has <%= live_series %> live series. <%= single_book_series %> hold a single book, and <%= books_in_several_series %> books are in more than one series. Work through them.

Why this matters: the site will later show, on a book's page, which series it belongs to and its position in it ("Book 3 of ..."). So the titles, the membership and the positions have to be right enough to show to readers. We want the legitimate series, and none of the junk.

What a legitimate series is: a set of books meant to be read as one sequence or one story world. The same characters, story or setting carried across books. Harry Potter, A Song of Ice and Fire, a romance series following one family, a manga run, a shared-world line such as Star Wars Legends.

Rules:

- A publisher line, imprint, format or subject collection is not a series, even when Goodreads calls it one ("Very Short Introductions", "Penguin Classics", "O'Reilly Pocket Reference", "Civil War America"). Delete it.
- Two rows that are the same series: merge them into the one with the better title and more books.
- A legitimate series with one book in our catalogue is fine. Keep it.
- The title is the series' usual English name, the one an English-language reader would recognise. Rename `夏目友人帳 [Natsume Yūjinchō]` to its English title when one is in common use, and drop Goodreads-only decorations.
- Positions are the reading order readers expect. Numbered entries get their number. A prequel can be 0 or a decimal such as 0.5. Companion books or novellas that are not part of the count are unnumbered, with a `position_label` if Goodreads gives one.

Tools:

- `list_admin_series` lists series. Filters: `q` (title substring), `min_books`, `max_books`, `include_deleted`; sort by `title`, `book_count` or `id`. Start with `max_books=1` and `min_books=1`, since single-book series are where most junk is. Also look for titles that read like publisher lines. Page through the whole list.
- `get_admin_series` shows one series with its books, their authors and their positions. `get_admin_book` shows which series a book is in. Use both before you decide.
- `create_admin_series` (optional `goodreads_series_id`), `update_admin_series` (`title`, `representative_book_id`, and `goodreads_series_id` on a series that has none), `add_series_book`, `update_series_book` and `remove_series_book` (keyed by book id) fix the contents.
- A book in several series: usually one of them is a publisher line or imprint. Check each with `get_admin_series` and delete the one that is not a real series.
- `delete_admin_series` and `merge_admin_series` take `dry_run`. In `merge_admin_series`, `id` is the series that survives and `source_ids` lists 1 to 50 series folded into it. Check you have not swapped them.

Workflow:

1. Call `dry_run: true` first on every `delete_admin_series` and `merge_admin_series`, read what it says would happen, and only then run it for real.
2. Deletes and merges stick: the Goodreads enrichment follows a merge and skips a deleted series, so it will not bring either back. For a junk series, delete the whole series rather than removing its books one by one. Removing a single book from a series that stays live may be undone, because the next enrichment of that book re-links it if Goodreads still lists it there.
3. Before `create_admin_series`, search with `list_admin_series q=...` and with `search_admin_books` to check the series and its books are not already there. Pass the Goodreads series id when you know it. Another series holding that id refuses the create.
4. A deleted or merged series cannot be edited. Take every id from the tools, never from memory.

You are authorised to act without asking me. If you genuinely cannot tell whether something is a legitimate series, leave it alone and say why.

When you finish, report what you changed: for each series, its id, what you did (deleted, merged into which id, renamed, books added, moved or removed) and one line of reasoning, plus what you left alone and why.
